today i'm going to be speaking with  jamal greene who teaches constitutional  law  at columbia university and also is  author of  how rights went wrong why our obsession  with rights  is tearing america apart it's so great  to have you on i appreciate it  good to be here thanks so maybe just to  frame our conversation you know one of  the things  i've talked about over the last few  years is that a lot of these movements  that um claim to be or are ostensibly  about rights and about uh staking a  claim to a right  exercising a certain right they seem to  do this in the absence of an  understanding that with rights come  responsibilities and that the claiming  of rights in a vacuum seems to be  untenable and in some cases an  impossibility sort of in modern modern  society i'd love to kind of first hear  your thoughts a little bit about that  and the idea of  are right something that we should be  exploring  in a vacuum absent any cultural societal  reality  or is that context absolutely crucial to  thinking about rights  well i certainly think that rights are  embedded within a society  and and we actually have a very long  tradition in the united states  of thinking about rights and their  limits as something that  is a collective exercise that's actually  how the framers thought about rights but  we've gone away from that  vision and gotten to a place where  and i think this is where your political  movements come in  we tend to to use rights as political  leverage  because we tend to think about them in  almost absolute terms as as you say in a  vacuum kind of disconnected from all  else  that once you claim a right then you get  to win right so the whole game  becomes can i claim a right can i get  relevant decision makers um sometimes  courts  to to say that i have a right and that's  what's going to win me  my political battle and we i think we  need to get back to thinking about  rights  in political terms which is to say in  terms of  of being part of a society what is a  framework where we can think about  situations where rights  seemingly conflict or when we're talking  about  one's freedom or right to do something  versus someone else's freedom or right  to be free of that something what what's  the framework that we should be applying  to those conflicts  well one of the things i emphasize in  the book is that rights  inherently conflict they always conflict  and you can if you think about your  right to form a society and to pass laws  right so government  is is designed to protect rights and  there's also of course another side  where there's individuals who are  claiming  particular rights against what  government does so there's constantly  conflict  you've got to have a strategy for  managing that conflict  in the us our legal system has developed  to a place where  the way we the way we manage it is to  kind of pretend it doesn't exist pretend  the conflict isn't there  and that one side is right and the other  side is wrong so you got to pick who has  the right in any particular case  whereas in actually many other countries  in fact most other countries  the way in which the legal system tends  to respond is through i'll use a  legalistic term called proportionality  but what it really means  is that you kind of background  the question of who has the right you  just assume that rights exist  on both sides of a lot of legal  questions and you focus much more  heavily on what is the government doing  what kind of problem is it trying to  respond to  what kind of evidence is it relying on  what kind of alternatives are available  to it  is it acting totally disproportionately  or is it acting basically reasonably  those are the kinds of questions that we  typically ask when we're asking about  is this just is this fair um and those  questions are totally consistent with  the  the notion that uh that their rights  involved uh because you've gotta  what i say in the book is mediate  between different claims of rights on  multiple sides of a conflict and get at  really factual questions of what's  what's actually going on here we don't  need to to get into kind of a  legalistic questions of what does the  first amendment mean or what does the  constitution say those questions don't  get us very far  in answering questions about rights  conflicts we've got to get into the  into the factual details so i mean to  take a case maybe many in my audience  will be familiar with this infamous  gay wedding cake case from from the  state of colorado  one interpretation of that would be that  it's a question of  the right of a business owner to make  decisions  about how they want to operate their  business and the right  of the couple wanting the cake not to be  discriminated against on the basis of  sexual orientation  it might it could be framed as a right  versus a right  all sorts of different participants have  reframed it  where there's other ways to to think  about it  one could say well it's a judge or jury  choosing as to which whose rights reign  supreme but is it not that simple  i think it's not that simple and it's a  it's a fascinating case because i think  it really does  kind of burrow down on this on this  problem where i actually think there's  even more than two um sets of rights  involved so there's the rights of a  baker who wants to  to decide which which weddings he bakes  cakes for right and he's claiming a  religious right and also a free speech  right because his cakes are really  artistic so he  right he thinks that it's a way of  speaking there's the right of the couple  to not be discriminated against as you  say david  uh and then there's the right of the  people of colorado passed a law that  says don't discriminate on the basis of  sexual orientation so  they've got right too at least the way i  frame it so there's rights on all sides  and you could think of it  in the most kind of binary terms as  a judge or jury's got to pick you know  whose rights count  but there actually are a lot  lots of overlap in how each of the sides  in that case understood the rights of  the other side  so the couple actually believed that a  a baker can choose whether to bake a  cake for someone he ideologically  disagrees with he can decide not to bake  a cake the couple agreed with that  the baker agreed that he actually can't  discriminate on the basis of sexual  orientation  uh in in baking his cakes he didn't  claim a first amendment right to avoid  discrimination  what the disagreement was was a much  more narrow disagreement  about whether refusing to bake a cake  for a same-sex wedding  counted as as as discrimination on the  basis of sexual orientation  because the baker said i don't care who  my customers are i just don't want to  participate in this wedding and so  that's an ideological  dispute according to him he's not  actually discriminating against any  people  and the couple said well no if this had  been a a  opposite sex wedding you would have you  would have baked the cake and so that is  discrimination but that's actually a  pretty narrow  conflict and what i'm trying to suggest  in the book is that judges really  need to focus on on the narrow  part of the conflict rather than trying  to decide you know do we care more about  religious freedom or do we care more  about  equality for same-sex couples those are  values conflicts that we're not going to  resolve in a court case and  shouldn't try to resolve but we can  actually get much closer together  if we focus on individual details in  fact i guess that's the tricky thing  where  it's very common that you will have a  case  that starts with what appears to be a  decision  or a conflict between some broader  values about how society or an economy  or a culture should operate  and by the time it gets to either the  initial court decision or even on an  appeal  some subsequent court's decision it ends  up being  something very very narrow and i think  to some degree that is a source of  frustration for many people  who would like to have these broader  issues resolved sort of like once and  for all  and it doesn't happen because the courts  end up very narrowly making  making a decision as you point out how  should the broader  questions be be dealt with because when  they're dealt with  so-called democratically either by by  you know a ballot initiative or by the  legislatures that we elect  um often it's still not permanent in  that  then it's undone uh at the next  opportunity i mean like how should a  society deal with the broader question  well the the broader questions of values  conflicts i think the first thing i'll  say is they shouldn't be resolved by  courts  okay and and this is the this is  rights conflicts are ultimately  political conflicts or conflicts between  people who have fundamental  disagreements with one another  and it's okay to have fundamental  disagreements with one another that's  what makes us  um human and in some ways the core of  the book is to say look the reason we  we disagree about rights is not because  one of us  is right and the other is wrong because  some of us are angels and others devils  because we're different  people we have different values  different commitments and  when you when you overlay a legal system  onto that kind of disagreement  the legal system has to figure out  how do you construct rules that enable  people to live together  and to stay invested within the  constitutional system so i actually  celebrate  uncertainty at least to a degree i  celebrate  not resolving rights conflicts for all  time because that  that that means that the constitution  doesn't protect or doesn't care about  one or the other side of really  important values conflicts  i don't think that's the way a legal  system should operate i think you try to  mediate those  conflicts through politics and when i  say politics i don't always mean  legislative politics or ballot  initiatives  i mean 