ts but they just don't  necessarily think of rights as kind of  trumps that kind of  overcome everything else and that's  what's really i think different about  our political culture  and i i think that i try to tell the  story in the  in the book and kind of the first third  of the book uh it doesn't come from the  founding which is what we often tell  ourselves the founders  had a much more communitarian view of  rights than  uh than modern americans do it really  comes out of the 1960s in the post-civil  rights era legacy  and it's a it's a it's a complex story  but what it boils down to  is is a history with race and  with the particular pathologies the  particular problem  of of people who are being racially  discriminated against in overt  ways being kind of the paradigm of  rights  in america right the the the core right  is the right against racial  discrimination  and that's not something you mediate  right that's not something where you say  well  you know let's see what the  segregationists think and then we'll  kind of see what the  other people think it doesn't lend  itself to mediation because one side of  that dispute  is completely bad faith oppressive um  abusive um actor but you can't  dissociate  all of government with you know anytime  my right is at stake that means the  government's being abusive or oppressive  um that's not most rights um that's not  most rights conflicts and so  i think we need a language in which we  can talk about ordinary political  conflict  that doesn't take the form of this is  let's see if this is just like jim crow  or let's see if this is just like  mccarthyism we've got to take  rights conflicts on their facts and  actually think about what the  government's trying to accomplish  and whether it's something that is  considered to be legitimate within our  tradition  very uh very well said uh the book as  we've been uh  mentioning is how rights went wrong why  our obsession with rights is tearing  america apart  we've been uh talking with the author of  the book  professor jamal green it's so great to  have you on i really appreciate your  time  great to be here i enjoyed the  conversation thanks

